          "text": "{\"translation_en\":null,\"larger_context_summary\":\"Jane narrates her observations of St John Rivers and Rosamond Oliver, the wealthy young woman he loves. Rosamond openly encourages him, and Jane believes that she returns—or would accept—his affection. St John nevertheless suppresses his feelings because he has dedicated himself to religious and missionary work and judges Rosamond unsuited to the life he intends. The imagined speech translates his involuntary physical reactions into words for the reader: his love is evident to Rosamond and Jane even though he refuses to voice it or propose marriage. The immediate stakes are therefore both romantic and vocational: personal happiness is sacrificed to what St John regards as a sacred calling.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The occurrence strongly presents love as an enduring inner state: St John’s trembling hand, burning eye, and inability to hide his response make the narrator’s verbal reconstruction highly emphatic. Yet the declaration is not actually spoken; Jane revoices what his look “seemed to say.” That mediation is valuable because it shows how fiction can make nonverbal conduct carry the semantic force of an avowal without converting it into literal dialogue. The emotion’s intensity does not make the expression a reflexive outburst: the imagined wording is controlled and explanatory, while St John’s visible body betrays what his will suppresses. Nor does it perform a relational commitment. The continuation explicitly keeps any offer counterfactual—“If I offered my heart”—and depicts that heart as already consecrated to his vocation. Thus love is presented as true and mutually consequential, but no proposal, promise, or new relationship is enacted.\"}"
